    {band: "B", title: "A Family Honey Business", text: "Dan's family has kept bees on their farm for three generations. Every spring they take the honey from the hives and sell it at a small stand by the road. Last year, Dan had an idea: he designed simple labels and started selling the honey online. Orders increased quickly, especially from people who wanted local products. Dan still works on the farm early in the morning, but in the afternoon he packs boxes and answers customers' messages. His mother still sells the honey at the stand on weekends, and the children help pack the jars.", questions: [
      {q: "How long has the family kept bees?", options: ["One generation", "Two generations", "Three generations", "Five generations"], answer: 2},
      {q: "When does the family take the honey from the hives?", options: ["In winter", "In spring", "In summer", "In autumn"], answer: 1},
      {q: "What did Dan start doing last year?", options: ["Keeping cows", "Selling honey online", "Opening a restaurant", "Teaching beekeeping"], answer: 1},
      {q: "Why did the orders increase?", options: ["The price went down", "Many people wanted local products", "The farm was famous", "A TV show mentioned it"], answer: 1},
      {q: "What does Dan do in the afternoon?", options: ["He sleeps", "He packs boxes and answers messages", "He milks the cows", "He drives to the market"], answer: 1}]},
    {band: "B", title: "A Second Career", text: "After he retired from his job as an accountant, Yosef felt bored at home. His daughter suggested that he try teaching mathematics to students in a community center. At first he was nervous, because he had not taught before. However, he soon discovered that he enjoyed explaining difficult ideas in simple words. Today he teaches three groups every week, and several of his students have improved their grades. Yosef says that he feels younger than he did when he worked in the office. His students call him the patient teacher, and he now leads a study group for adults on Saturday mornings.", questions: [
      {q: "What was Yosef's job before he retired?", options: ["Teacher", "Accountant", "Engineer", "Doctor"], answer: 1},
      {q: "Why did he feel bored?", options: ["He had no friends", "He was at home too much", "He had no money", "He was sick"], answer: 1},
      {q: "Where does he teach?", options: ["At a university", "In a community center", "At his daughter's school", "In an office"], answer: 1},
      {q: "How did Yosef feel at first about teaching?", options: ["Very excited", "Nervous", "Angry", "Relaxed"], answer: 1},
      {q: "What did he discover about himself?", options: ["He hated mathematics", "He enjoyed explaining difficult ideas", "He wanted to be an accountant again", "He could not speak clearly"], answer: 1}]},
    {band: "B", title: "The Lost Trail", text: "Noa and her friend Rina planned a short hike in the north. They followed a map they had printed at home, but after two hours they reached a river that was not on the paper. The path had been closed because of a recent landslide, and the sign was hidden behind a bush. They decided to walk back the way they had come, which took longer than they expected. When they finally reached the parking lot, they called the park office to report the problem. Noa said she would bring a GPS device next time. Later, the two friends wrote a short report for other hikers, so that they would know about the closed path.", questions: [
      {q: "What did Noa and Rina plan to do?", options: ["Go shopping", "Take a short hike", "Swim in the sea", "Ride bikes"], answer: 1},
      {q: "What did they find after two hours?", options: ["A village", "A river not on the map", "A restaurant", "Their car"], answer: 1},
      {q: "Why was the path closed?", options: ["Because of a landslide", "Because of a festival", "Because of heavy traffic", "Because it was night"], answer: 0},
      {q: "What did they do after they got lost?", options: ["They walked back", "They waited for help", "They took a taxi", "They slept in the park"], answer: 0},
      {q: "What will Noa bring next time?", options: ["A GPS device", "A tent", "A bicycle", "Extra food"], answer: 0}]},
    {band: "C", title: "The Disappearing Bees", text: "Across the continent, the number of wild bees has fallen sharply over the past decades. Researchers point to several causes, including the loss of flowering plants, the spread of certain pesticides, and changes in climate that disrupt the timing between flowers and insects. Farmers who rely on pollination now face higher costs, since they must sometimes bring in hives from distant regions. Some scientists argue that planting wildflower strips along field edges could reverse the trend, although the evidence is still limited and more long-term studies are required. Beekeepers in several countries have started sharing data about their hives, which may help researchers see the pattern more clearly.", questions: [
      {q: "What has happened to wild bees?", options: ["Their number has risen", "Their number has fallen sharply", "They moved to cities", "They became larger"], answer: 1},
      {q: "Which factor is mentioned as a cause?", options: ["The loss of flowering plants", "A rise in the price of honey", "Too many farmers", "Longer nights"], answer: 0},
      {q: "Why do farmers face higher costs?", options: ["They must bring in hives from distant regions", "They must buy new land", "Bees attack their crops", "The government charges tax"], answer: 0},
      {q: "What do some scientists suggest?", options: ["Building more roads", "Planting wildflower strips along field edges", "Banning all farming", "Importing foreign bees"], answer: 1},
      {q: "How does the text describe the evidence for this idea?", options: ["Complete and proven", "Still limited", "Completely wrong", "Based on a single farm"], answer: 1}]},
    {band: "C", title: "Remote Work and the City Center", text: "Since the pandemic, many companies have allowed employees to work from home for part of the week. This shift has changed the daily life of city centers. Cafés that once depended on office workers now struggle to attract customers on Mondays and Fridays, while residential neighborhoods have grown busier during the day. Urban planners now debate whether offices should be converted into apartments or turned into shared workspaces. Critics warn that a long-term decline in office use could reduce tax income for municipalities that rely on commercial property. Some cities now offer tax breaks to companies that keep their offices open for part of the week.", questions: [
      {q: "What has changed since the pandemic?", options: ["Employees work from home for part of the week", "Offices closed forever", "Cities became smaller", "Trains became free"], answer: 0},
      {q: "Why do cafés near offices struggle?", options: ["Their coffee became expensive", "Office workers are absent on some days", "The government banned them", "Customers prefer tea"], answer: 1},
      {q: "What do urban planners debate?", options: ["Whether to build more highways", "Whether offices should become apartments or shared workspaces", "Whether to close schools", "Whether to remove parks"], answer: 1},
      {q: "What do critics warn about?", options: ["Rising food prices", "A fall in tax income for some municipalities", "Traffic accidents", "Lower school attendance"], answer: 1},
      {q: "What happened to residential neighborhoods?", options: ["They became quieter during the day", "They grew busier during the day", "They were abandoned", "They were moved to the suburbs"], answer: 1}]},
    {band: "C", title: "The Letter in the Attic", text: "While clearing an old attic, a family found a bundle of letters written in 1943 by a young nurse to her brother. The letters described long shifts in a field hospital, shortages of medicine, and small moments of kindness between soldiers and staff. Historians who examined the bundle noticed that several letters had been censored, with whole lines blacked out. They believe the cuts were made by military officials. The family has decided to donate the letters to a museum, where they will be preserved and displayed next spring. Before the move, the museum will copy every page, so that researchers can study the handwriting and the notes in the margins.", questions: [
      {q: "When were the letters written?", options: ["1903", "1943", "1963", "1993"], answer: 1},
      {q: "Who wrote the letters?", options: ["A soldier", "A young nurse", "A doctor", "A historian"], answer: 1},
      {q: "What did historians notice about the letters?", options: ["Several had been censored", "They were written in English", "Some were lost", "They were signed by the king"], answer: 0},
      {q: "Who do historians believe made the cuts?", options: ["The family", "The museum staff", "Military officials", "The nurse herself"], answer: 2},
      {q: "What does the family plan to do with the letters?", options: ["Sell them", "Donate them to a museum", "Burn them", "Keep them in the attic"], answer: 1}]},
    {band: "C", title: "Why Habits Stick", text: "Psychologists who study habits have found that most daily routines are formed in stable contexts. When an action is repeated in the same place and at the same time, the brain begins to link the situation with the behavior, so the action requires less conscious effort. Breaking an old habit is harder, because the cues that trigger it remain present. Some researchers suggest that changing the environment, rather than relying on willpower alone, is the most effective strategy. Removing sweets from the kitchen, for example, can reduce late-night snacking more than resolutions made in the morning. The same idea applies to phone notifications: when they are turned off in the bedroom, it is easier to sleep.", questions: [
      {q: "What makes a routine become automatic?", options: ["Repeating it in the same place and time", "Changing it every day", "Writing about it", "Talking to others about it"], answer: 0},
      {q: "Why is breaking a habit hard?", options: ["The cues that trigger it remain present", "The brain forgets quickly", "People get bored with food", "Habits are always useful"], answer: 0},
      {q: "What do some researchers consider most effective?", options: ["Relying on willpower alone", "Changing the environment", "Making morning resolutions", "Taking long walks"], answer: 1},
      {q: "What example does the text give?", options: ["Removing sweets from the kitchen", "Buying a new car", "Joining a gym", "Writing a diary"], answer: 0},
      {q: "How can removing sweets help, according to the text?", options: ["It reduces late-night snacking more than resolutions", "It increases the cost of food", "It improves sleep alone", "It makes people angry"], answer: 0}]},
    {band: "C", title: "Machines in the Newsroom", text: "Several news organizations have begun using automated systems to write short reports on earnings, sports results, and weather. Editors say the software saves time and frees reporters for longer investigations. However, critics argue that automated articles can repeat errors from their data sources without any human check. Some journalists fear that cheap automated content will reduce the number of jobs in the field. In response, many newsrooms now require that a human editor review every automated story before it is published, and they label such articles clearly for readers. Some newsrooms also publish short notes that explain which parts of a story were written by software.", questions: [
      {q: "What do automated systems write about?", options: ["Short reports on earnings, sports, and weather", "Long investigations", "Opinion columns", "Novels"], answer: 0},
      {q: "What do editors say the software does?", options: ["It saves time and frees reporters for longer investigations", "It replaces all editors", "It writes headlines only", "It removes all errors"], answer: 0},
      {q: "What do critics worry about?", options: ["Automated articles can repeat errors without a human check", "Reporters are too slow", "Readers do not read news", "Data is too expensive"], answer: 0},
      {q: "What do some journalists fear?", options: ["That automated content will reduce jobs", "That readers will stop buying newspapers", "That software will become too expensive", "That editors will retire"], answer: 0},
      {q: "What do many newsrooms now require?", options: ["Reporters write every story by hand", "Editors work only on weekends", "Automated stories are published without labels", "A human editor reviews each automated story"], answer: 3}]}
